**Definition 2.1 (the unchanged original class).** For a valid base
$b=(X,Y,Z)$ set $r=1-Z$ and

$$
\begin{aligned}
&0<Z<1,\qquad 0<X,Y<r,\\
&J_b=(\ell_b,u_b)=(\max(0,X+Y-r),\min(X,Y)),\\
&p_b(k)=(r-X-Y+k,X-k,Z,Y-k,k),\qquad
\mathbf d=(1,-1,0,-1,1),\\
&I_b(k)=\sum_{i\in\{0,1,3,4\}}p_b(k)_i^{-1},\qquad
w_b(k)_i=\frac{\mathbf d_i}{p_b(k)_i I_b(k)}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{FH.1}
$$

The original mode order is $(0,1,2,3,13)$; index $4$ means mode $13$.
An original control has finite $T\geq0$, continuous real $k,\chi$ on
$[0,T]$, strict $k(t)\in J_b$, finite variation of $\chi$, and either
absolute continuity of both coordinates or continuous finite-piecewise-$C^1$
coordinates whose derivative rates extend continuously to each closed
partition piece. Repeated partition times are permitted. Integrating those
rates and telescoping proves absolute continuity in the latter case too.
For a common compatible preparation lift $\theta$, the actual state uses

$$
H_i(t)=\int_0^t w_b(k(s))_i\chi'(s)\,ds,
\qquad
\psi_i(t)=\sqrt{p_b(k(t))_i}\,e^{-i(\theta_i+H_i(t))}.
\tag{FH.2}
$$

The real lift precedes any reduction modulo $2\pi$. Every signed integer
$m$ in a loop increment $2\pi m$ remains in the source history. The
double-wall examples below do not replace other bases, either regularity
alternative, any finite duration or any signed sector of this original class.

